        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Distinguish between facts and opinions in texts on everyday subjects. Reading: Analyze the similarities and differences between two texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can distinguish between factual information and opinions in a travel guide and explain how these impact visitor perceptions (e.g., "The guide says Panama hats are popular souvenirs, but this might be an opinion to promote local businesses."). Can compare two guides on Panama City attractions (e.g., "Both guides say you have to try the restaurants. The local food is amazing."). Can analyze opinion differences between two restaurant reviews (e.g., "One reviewer loved the food, but didn't like the decorations. The other reviewer loved everything about the restaurant.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images showing famous Panamanian landmarks and attractions (e.g., Casco Viejo, Biomuseo, Panama Canal, a local beach, a vibrant market). Students work in pairs to identify the places and share one "unique fact" or something "interesting" they know about each. They can use vocabulary such as "attraction," "landmark," "history," "museum," "beach," "market."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the difference between a fact and an opinion using an example from the warm-up visuals. "The Panama Canal connects the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans. (Fact) I think it's a breathtaking engineering wonder. (Opinion)." The teacher also models the pronunciation of "interesting" (/ˈɪn.trə.stɪŋ/) and "restaurant" (/ˈres.trɒnt/) emphasizing reduced syllables.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can an opinion be proven true or false?" (No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a fact always true, no matter who says it?" (Yes)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting Our Country".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Two short travel guide excerpts (e.g., "Panama City: A Traveler's Gem" and "Exploring Casco Viejo") about local Panamanian attractions, focusing on Casco Viejo. The texts are B1.1 level, containing both factual information (history, location, blocks, cathedral, market, transportation) and subjective opinions (must-see, wonderful, unique, breathtaking, tranquil, interesting, recommend, enjoy).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Can distinguish between factual information and opinions in a travel guide and explain how these impact visitor perceptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the titles and quickly skim the texts. <b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main purpose of these two texts?" (To describe and promote visiting Casco Viejo).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching Activity:</b> Students match key vocabulary words from the texts (e.g., "cathedral," "blocks," "market," "history," "restaurant") with short definitions or images.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> The teacher provides 3-4 True/False statements about the texts, some factual (e.g., "Casco Viejo is located in Panama City.") and some opinion-based (e.g., "The guide says the food market is the best place to eat."). Students identify if the statement is T or F and indicate if it's a fact or an opinion.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Distinguishing facts from opinions in travel-related texts. Analyzing similarities and differences between two texts. Using Past Simple/Present Perfect, Future with "will"/"going to," and Reported Speech. Applying vocabulary related to travel and local attractions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Fact vs. Opinion identification):</b> Students reread the two travel guide excerpts. Individually, they use two different colored highlighters (e.g., blue for facts, yellow for opinions) to mark sentences or phrases in the texts. The teacher circulates, offering support and asking guiding questions like, "How do you know that's a fact/opinion?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Grammar and Vocabulary in context):</b> Students complete sentences based on the texts, using target grammar and vocabulary.</li>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Casco Viejo (be) _____ once a neglected area, but after restoration, it (become) _____ a popular destination for tourists." (Past Simple/Present Perfect)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The first guide (state) _____ that the architecture (be) _____ unique and breathtaking." (Reported Speech, Vocabulary)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"They (recommend) _____ trying the local seafood, so we (go) _____ to the market tomorrow." (Reported Speech, Future with 'going to', Vocabulary)</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Comparison (Text Analysis and Pragmatic Function):</b> In pairs, students create a Venn diagram or T-chart to compare the two travel guides. They identify:</li>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Similar facts mentioned in both guides (e.g., location, history, landmarks like the cathedral).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Different opinions or emphasis in each guide (e.g., one focusing more on nightlife, the other on cultural museums).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Specific adjectives (breathtaking, tranquil, must-see, wonderful) and verbs (explore, showcase, enjoy) used in each text.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They also discuss: "What places should my friend visit based on these guides?" (Pragmatic function: Asking for recommendations).</li>
                    </ul>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting Our Country".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group discussion and presentation (3-4 students per group).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> Students imagine they are travel advisors helping a friend plan a "visit" to Casco Viejo. Each group receives two different online reviews (or blog post excerpts) of a specific restaurant or attraction within Casco Viejo (e.g., "Café Del Mar" or "National Theatre"). One review is largely positive, and the other is mixed or slightly negative. Groups must:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Read both texts carefully, distinguishing facts from opinions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Discuss the similarities and differences in the opinions expressed by the reviewers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prepare a short (2-3 minute) oral summary and recommendation for their friend, integrating information from both reviews.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They should use reported speech to summarize reviewers' comments (e.g., "One reviewer said that the atmosphere was fantastic, but the other reported that the service was slow.") and future tense to offer advice (e.g., "I think we will go there for dinner, but we're going to make a reservation to avoid long waits."). They also describe the landmarks (e.g., "The theatre showcases our cultural heritage.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups should also make suggestions and offer directions as part of their plan (Sociolinguistic competence). For example, "Let's plan the trip so we visit the museum in the morning. After that, we can walk through the blocks to the restaurant, which is opposite the main cathedral."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in analyzing texts, collaborative interaction, and effectively communicating recommendations while integrating new language content.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group presentations (Stage 4) and peer assessment using a simplified rubric for clarity and use of target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their group's summary and recommendation based on the two reviews. Peers listen and provide feedback on how well the presenting group distinguished facts/opinions and compared the texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurately distinguishes facts and opinions from the texts (Reading Objective).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clearly analyzes similarities and differences between the two texts (Reading Objective).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., unique, interesting, must-see, recommend, explore, enjoy, tranquil, breathtaking) effectively.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Applies appropriate grammar (Past Simple/Present Perfect, Future with "will"/"going to", Reported Speech).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participates actively and collaboratively in group planning and presentation (Sociolinguistic competence).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of contrastive sounds and reduced syllables is intelligible.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two reflective prompts:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of distinguishing between facts and opinions in the reviews?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How does understanding opinions in a travel guide or review help you make better decisions when planning a visit?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new unique facts or interesting details did you learn about local attractions in Panama today from reading the texts?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How will applying these reading skills (fact/opinion, comparing texts) help you understand other types of information, like news articles or social media posts?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The lesson successfully engaged students in distinguishing facts and opinions, and comparing texts. The use of authentic travel guide excerpts provided a relevant context for the "Visiting Our Country" scenario. Most students were able to identify factual information, but some struggled with nuanced opinions, especially when they were subtly presented. The grammar practice with Past Simple/Present Perfect and Reported Speech was helpful for summarizing. The group presentation allowed for good practice of pragmatic and sociolinguistic functions. For Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), I need to design activities that specifically prompt students to express and defend their *own* opinions, using the vocabulary and grammar from this lesson, and practice asking for and giving recommendations verbally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the facts and opinions they've analyzed today to participate in a collaborative trip planning activity. They will verbally "advise" and "recommend" specific "attractions" and "restaurants" based on visitor profiles, practicing asking for recommendations on local attractions and describing popular landmarks, ensuring everyone understands the "plan" and "itinerary."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>